{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: review the browser work-count repair at exact clean head 804bcd1f9af3cc050733688d3f7186e466aacbb6, directly citing artifact c434cdd076e494426ff039b083809491089283a0. Do not merge.\n\nPriority 2 of 6. Hugh reported this directly — \"the browser UI shows the wrong numbers\" — so it is a user-visible defect on the surface he reads, which puts it above the three infrastructure heads below.\n\nThe number he saw was 413 in the header chip, tooltipped \"413 need attention\". Nothing in the workroom is 413: it was 223 commitments carrying a stale or disputed qualifier plus 190 retired-or-stale artifacts, added together. `gs status` reports those populations on separate lines and never adds them.\n\nUnderneath the mixing was a second fault. Attention was treated as a third slice beside Active and Closed, when staleness and dispute are qualifiers on top of a lifecycle status: 188 of the 223 were already counted as active or closed, so the three figures exceeded the number of commitments. A third inconsistency sat beside it — Active was read from the projection while Attention and Closed were summed over topics.\n\nStatus partitions cleanly, which is what made this a fix rather than a compromise: 75 active, 411 closed and 35 lifecycle-stale sum to exactly the 521 commitments in the log. Attention is now reported as the overlapping qualifier it is, and artifacts are reported beside the commitment counts rather than inside them.\n\nTwo things to judge rather than take from me. I changed an existing test that asserted the mixed count as correct — it encoded the defect, so it changed with the behaviour, and I would rather you look at that directly than find it in a commit that also changes what the test measures. And the earlier request framed this as an open product choice between comparable numbers and keeping the artifact signal; I took neither horn because the arithmetic allowed both, which was luck rather than judgement.\n\nGates: `npx tsc --n